# Pipeline Stages

## 1. Requirement

Define the problem to solve.

Identify objectives.

Determine expected outcomes.

---

## 2. Environment Analysis

Observe the existing system.

Collect runtime information.

Determine constraints.

Identify dependencies.

No modifications occur during observation.

---

## 3. Planning

Define engineering strategy.

Select workflows.

Determine implementation sequence.

Identify verification points.

---

## 4. Architecture Design

Define system structure.

Separate presentation from logic.

Establish runtime boundaries.

Identify bridge and state files.

Plan expansion points.

---

## 5. Model Selection

Select the appropriate AI models.

Determine routing strategy.

Configure hybrid model usage.

Assign specialized responsibilities.

---

## 6. Runtime Selection

Choose reusable runtime modules.

Examples include:

Routing Runtime

Memory Runtime

Clone Runtime

Compatibility Runtime

Package Runtime

Database Runtime

Security Runtime

Event Runtime

Observation Runtime

Only verified runtimes are introduced.

---

## 7. Memory Integration

Configure memory layers.

User Memory

Project Memory

Session Memory

Knowledge Memory

Bridge Memory

State remains explicit.

---

## 8. Asset Creation

Create engineering documentation.

Architecture diagrams.

Runtime maps.

Protocol documentation.

Visual assets.

User documentation.

Assets become part of the engineering record.

---

## 9. Development

Implement functionality.

Maintain module separation.

Avoid duplicate logic.

Respect runtime boundaries.

---

## 10. Verification

Verify before expansion.

Run validation.

Inspect outputs.

Confirm state integrity.

Observe results before proceeding.

---

## 11. Testing

Functional testing.

Runtime validation.

Integration testing.

Regression testing.

Compatibility testing.

Observation testing.

---

## 12. Documentation

Generate documentation.

Engineering references.

API documentation.

Runtime descriptions.

Migration notes.

Release notes.

Documentation is considered part of the product.

---

## 13. Packaging

Assemble release artifacts.

Executable packages.

Portable distributions.

Documentation bundles.

Media assets.

Installation resources.

---

## 14. Deployment

Release verified software.

Distribute packages.

Publish documentation.

Register releases.

Maintain reproducibility.

---

## 15. Observation

Monitor runtime behavior.

Collect telemetry.

Review reports.

Capture engineering observations.

No expansion occurs until observation is complete.

---

## 16. Feedback

Analyze observations.

Identify improvements.

Capture user feedback.

Review engineering metrics.

Determine future work.

---

## 17. Expansion

Introduce new functionality only after verification.

Maintain backward compatibility.

Preserve stable systems.

Repeat the Software Factory Pipeline.

